<resources>

    <!--
      The same three messages the favorites and category lists show, word for word: one broken sound
      should not read three ways depending on which screen the listener is looking at. They used to
      say "audio" here, which named the format rather than the thing, and had already half drifted
      back — the third one said "sound" under an `audio_` key.

      Declared per feature rather than shared: the design system draws the line but deliberately
      does not choose the words, which differ for a sound and for a story.
    -->
    <string name="sound_not_found">This sound is no longer in the catalog</string>
    <string name="sound_unavailable">Could not reach this sound. Tap play to try again.</string>
    <string name="sound_could_not_open">Could not play this sound</string>

    <!--
      Named for what it is, because it is not the only volume in play. The device's own media
      volume multiplies with this one, and the phone's volume keys move that one — so a listener
      who has silenced their device sees this slider still reading 100% and hears nothing. The
      name and the line below it are what say so; the two stay separate deliberately, because
      turning a sleep sound down should not turn the whole phone down.
    -->
    <string name="volume">Sound volume</string>
    <string name="volume_is_separate">Separate from your device volume</string>
    <!--
      One `%`, not the `%%` an Android XML string would escape it with: Compose Multiplatform
      formats this itself and passes a doubled sign straight through, so the slider read "100%%"
      on a device from the first commit until someone looked at one.
    -->
    <string name="volume_percentage">%1$d%</string>
    <string name="loop_sound">Loop sound</string>
</resources>
